      "text": "Data Layout: Single Inheritance without Virtual Functions",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Data Layout: Single Inheritance without Virtual Functions",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "QFdUe1SyxXErBD3GotShK",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 440.8928113664899,
      "y": 617.3809676397414,
      "width": 1230.4029312133791,
      "height": 24.375,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#e03131",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "dotted",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": "pkDyDOfbi4IQsej1baoX3",
      "index": "b3lzV",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 124630105,
      "version": 576,
      "versionNonce": 387065273,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1777292378668,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "What are the possible pitfalls of transforming two independent classes into a type/subtype relationship through inheritance?",
      "fontSize": 19.5,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "What are the possible pitfalls of transforming two independent classes into a type/subtype relationship through inheritance?",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "7tr_BfF17jOvFSjfV5bR6",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 433.39281136649004,
      "y": 666.1309676397414,
      "width": 1246.9692382812502,
      "height": 175,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#2f9e44",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "dotted",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": "pkDyDOfbi4IQsej1baoX3",
      "index": "b3lzl",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1695565625,
      "version": 689,
      "versionNonce": 920055383,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1777292378668,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. double the number of function calls to perform the same operations. That is, say the constructor or operator+=()\n in our example were not made inline (or the compiler could not for some reason support the inlining of the member functions).\nThe initialization or addition of a Point3d object would be the cost of the partial Point2d and Point3d instances. \n\n2. A second possible pitfall in factoring a class into a two-level or deeper hierarchy is a possible bloating of the space\n necessary to represent the abstraction as a class hierarchy. The issue is the language guarantee of the integrity of \nthe base class subobject within the derived class. ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. double the number of function calls to perform the same operations. That is, say the constructor or operator+=()\n in our example were not made inline (or the compiler could not for some reason support the inlining of the member functions). The initialization or addition of a Point3d object would be the cost of the partial Point2d and Point3d instances. \n\n2. A second possible pitfall in factoring a class into a two-level or deeper hierarchy is a possible bloating of the space\n necessary to represent the abstraction as a class hierarchy. The issue is the language guarantee of the integrity of \nthe base class subobject within the derived class. ",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
